#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89fdb32a-ab35-5efb-9f04-a3d2aae06d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338912} {:answer/id #uuid "349e272b-364e-5c69-9574-ea59a7341051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338913} {:answer/id #uuid "d6850a4b-b2db-5fde-94ea-9ebd2a16978a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338914} {:answer/id #uuid "83974c3e-a812-50dd-b4f5-9026726bd3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338915} {:answer/id #uuid "8f76844d-cf56-5d9e-9669-ca567d5c5658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f76844d-cf56-5d9e-9669-ca567d5c5658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338916} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ad2a274-384e-53de-ac30-9d0c29892133" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0328f15-f31e-587d-938d-d61590167bae" :question/math-academy-id "q-140040" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-9\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -32+36 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-2I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53fd241e-b38c-55d8-9f12-a12d08a0e16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338919} {:answer/id #uuid "353e424e-0ac2-568e-ae87-ff7bcb33a26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338920} {:answer/id #uuid "4de4da56-2e52-5786-8a37-4434e69b56db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338921} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d0f0fa-3911-5761-bd3c-7b85a16c8165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338922} {:answer/id #uuid "a792d964-068d-5f40-a5fd-e768ca2cf81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a792d964-068d-5f40-a5fd-e768ca2cf81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338923} :answer-field/id #uuid "c82c89df-a0fb-5a2f-8956-b3be5f6ac309" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c022e488-b03e-52fb-b544-84b03490d40c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36929" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-4I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94ed63b5-b327-504d-bfda-43d9514f5a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338925} {:answer/id #uuid "10c0c9e8-ac94-5335-a758-454e23405e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338926} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea89b01-e8ed-51fc-8222-dfac217f1cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338927} {:answer/id #uuid "2197da3a-6915-5b07-b387-6cc591b61ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338928} {:answer/id #uuid "23023d12-e7f5-5113-8699-afe549a47089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2197da3a-6915-5b07-b387-6cc591b61ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338928} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd825cc5-1816-5dd3-928a-c9fe7255f51a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f978364-b49e-551e-8d94-9dbc6a1be095" :question/math-academy-id "q-37634" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-8\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe74362-353d-5924-9358-45d39d94a180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338932} {:answer/id #uuid "0ebf8095-a684-5c4c-99ad-553456f51919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338933} {:answer/id #uuid "44eecb0a-d30c-5126-85fc-c561fe5c7b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338934} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ceb67b-95fc-5d21-a3ae-11502dc74115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338935} {:answer/id #uuid "7892ba22-d3ed-5a02-a5bd-57153777044b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44eecb0a-d30c-5126-85fc-c561fe5c7b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338934} :answer-field/id #uuid "06fa57d8-1995-5f95-b249-3df8a454a386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b873a918-b542-5b82-8f6e-b94782f43ab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-80464" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 2 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3c0f626-5892-5a52-80b4-8ced8f520502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338939} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6dfb09-d3a7-5cf3-ad9d-b5db55f5153a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338940} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f866f8-11c5-5336-a804-a58dc14462a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338941} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3d90b3-2aab-56a9-b56a-8990039213b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338942} {:answer/id #uuid "419fedc6-e1b5-5400-a395-35e2a75b196e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a6dfb09-d3a7-5cf3-ad9d-b5db55f5153a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338940} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc8dfc0-386e-5fe6-a862-155dc9f936d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "912d4ab2-e55a-5e9a-8ddf-b1e59d14eb18" :question/math-academy-id "q-80467" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$   find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$  \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$"}]}
